00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:11.925
And you may want to
keep the mic closer to--

00:10:11.925 --> 00:10:12.840
VIKRAM VERMA: Sure.

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:15.360
SALIL AMONKAR: So, we
talked about AI and ML,

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:17.860
and I presented the front
end and the back end

00:10:17.860 --> 00:10:19.370
of the processes.

00:10:19.370 --> 00:10:22.210
And then we talked about
a lost opportunity.

00:10:22.210 --> 00:10:24.610
And there's Mark
Anderson's quote,

00:10:24.610 --> 00:10:28.510
which is talks a little
bit about the same thing.

00:10:28.510 --> 00:10:30.769
What's your perspective?

00:10:30.769 --> 00:10:33.310
VIKRAM VERMA: At the very high
level, I agree with his quote.

00:10:33.310 --> 00:10:36.850
But at the same time, technology
is the enabler of the vision

00:10:36.850 --> 00:10:39.997
that we have to
put in the front.

00:10:39.997 --> 00:10:41.830
If we don't have the
vision, then technology

00:10:41.830 --> 00:10:45.530
is not going to help
us in the long run.

00:10:45.530 --> 00:10:48.370
There are a lot of examples
like Google, Amazon, all

00:10:48.370 --> 00:10:50.020
these companies
where technology was

00:10:50.020 --> 00:10:51.551
not there when they started it.

00:10:51.551 --> 00:10:53.800
So if the vision was not
there, and if the willingness

00:10:53.800 --> 00:10:56.980
of taking that vision to
reality was not there,

00:10:56.980 --> 00:10:58.546
technology would not help us.

00:10:58.546 --> 00:10:59.920
Now, there are a
lot of use cases

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:03.880
also where technology came
in, and as a human being,

00:11:03.880 --> 00:11:06.790
when we learn somebody
else doing something,

00:11:06.790 --> 00:11:11.930
we try to see how we can take
benefit of the same concept

00:11:11.930 --> 00:11:13.670
and evolve to the next level.

00:11:13.670 --> 00:11:17.260
So there are a lot of
examples in that as well.

00:11:17.260 --> 00:11:19.975
So it is a philosophical
debate, but it depends

00:11:19.975 --> 00:11:21.100
on how you want to take it.

00:11:21.100 --> 00:11:23.710
I think ideas and a
willingness to take

00:11:23.710 --> 00:11:26.409
those ideas to execution is
more important than technology.

00:11:26.409 --> 00:11:27.450
SALIL AMONKAR: Very good.

00:11:27.450 --> 00:11:28.920
Sudhakar, you want to
add anything to that.

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:30.045
SUDHAKAR PARAKALA: I agree.

00:11:30.045 --> 00:11:33.880
So the problem of the
demand-supply matching

00:11:33.880 --> 00:11:35.950
and time-to-market
were always there.

00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:39.070
So we had to look
into solutions that

00:11:39.070 --> 00:11:43.190
could help us to bridge the gap
as to optimize our inventory.

00:11:43.190 --> 00:11:46.060
So recently for the
last couple of years,

00:11:46.060 --> 00:11:49.370
we have realized
that there is ML,

00:11:49.370 --> 00:11:51.430
and there are
technological improvements

00:11:51.430 --> 00:11:56.380
wherein we could
leverage that technology

00:11:56.380 --> 00:11:57.400
to bridge those gaps.

00:11:57.400 --> 00:11:59.350
So now we think we are
at that point wherein

00:11:59.350 --> 00:12:02.240
we could leverage
ML significantly

00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:04.907
to achieve our goals.

00:12:04.907 --> 00:12:07.625
SALIL AMONKAR:
Adam, any comments?

00:12:07.625 --> 00:12:08.770
ADAM SPUNBERG: Sure.

00:12:08.770 --> 00:12:12.430
I think one thing
interesting about that quote,

00:12:12.430 --> 00:12:16.270
it focuses on the '90s as
if where we stand today,

00:12:16.270 --> 00:12:19.600
we've solved all the problems
that prevented those businesses

00:12:19.600 --> 00:12:21.130
from succeeding then.

00:12:21.130 --> 00:12:25.480
But I think this is a universal
construct that will probably

00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:27.340
continue to happen.

00:12:27.340 --> 00:12:30.340
And people with
great vision today

00:12:30.340 --> 00:12:32.470
may have ideas that
aren't possible now,

00:12:32.470 --> 00:12:36.440
but might be more available
in 10 to 15 years.

00:12:36.440 --> 00:12:38.920
So I'd go so far as to say
that I agree with his quote

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:42.790
and actually think it's
prophetic in some ways

00:12:42.790 --> 00:12:45.410
about how technology works.

00:12:45.410 --> 00:12:48.790
But as we advance, maybe
with such exponential growth,

00:12:48.790 --> 00:12:51.640
that gap will diminish.

00:12:51.640 --> 00:12:54.790
And we'll be closer and closer
between vision and technology.

00:12:58.064 --> 00:12:59.480
DEEPAK MEHROTRA:
I think what Adam

00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:02.830
said is so perfect, which is
exponential growth and bridging

00:13:02.830 --> 00:13:04.090
the gap.

00:13:04.090 --> 00:13:07.870
I don't think of anyone of us
in the room can imagine a world

00:13:07.870 --> 00:13:11.740
where only large companies with
deep pockets are going to rule.

00:13:11.740 --> 00:13:14.104
So the machines are here.

00:13:14.104 --> 00:13:16.270
Whether they're going to
take over the world or not,

00:13:16.270 --> 00:13:18.490
that's a philosophical
debate we can have.

00:13:18.490 --> 00:13:24.430
But as a business
that is not big--

00:13:24.430 --> 00:13:27.550
let's pick up a fight
with Amazon for now, which

00:13:27.550 --> 00:13:29.680
happens to be our
number one channel,

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:32.810
but also happens to
be our competition.

00:13:32.810 --> 00:13:38.050
The question is, what do we
do that we are in the race?

00:13:38.050 --> 00:13:39.830
We are not behind it.

00:13:39.830 --> 00:13:42.250
We can hopefully then compete.

00:13:42.250 --> 00:13:44.620
And how would we scale?

00:13:44.620 --> 00:13:46.030
How would we optimize?

00:13:46.030 --> 00:13:48.400
How would we reduce wastages?

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:52.670
So all those questions
for any business around,

00:13:52.670 --> 00:13:54.274
it's fairly simple.

00:13:54.274 --> 00:13:55.690
I mean, it's not
going to be easy.

00:13:55.690 --> 00:13:58.700
But if you're not in the
race, you'll be out of it.

00:13:58.700 --> 00:14:02.560
So with that in
mind, things are only

00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:05.750
going to get very interesting.

00:14:05.750 --> 00:14:11.970
SALIL AMONKAR: So following that
Vikram your comments earlier,

00:14:11.970 --> 00:14:13.800
how do you--

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:15.930
what is it about
that perspective

00:14:15.930 --> 00:14:18.060
that you would like
to relate in the work

00:14:18.060 --> 00:14:22.260
that, for example, you're
doing in your area or even

00:14:22.260 --> 00:14:26.580
from your thought processes
as it relates to marketing,

00:14:26.580 --> 00:14:27.730
and sales, and AI and ML?

00:14:27.730 --> 00:14:29.237
VIKRAM VERMA: Yeah, absolutely.

00:14:29.237 --> 00:14:31.320
There are a lot of scenarios
that we are currently

00:14:31.320 --> 00:14:32.790
working on.

00:14:32.790 --> 00:14:36.760
For example, when we started
this journey two years ago,

00:14:36.760 --> 00:14:40.350
it was always about
customer success motion.

00:14:40.350 --> 00:14:43.620
Once you bought
something, how do we

00:14:43.620 --> 00:14:45.840
drive the value of
that product as soon as

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:47.550
possible to the customer?

00:14:47.550 --> 00:14:49.500
And the key concept
over there was,

00:14:49.500 --> 00:14:51.780
how can we understand
what you are looking

00:14:51.780 --> 00:14:53.400
for from other software?

00:14:53.400 --> 00:14:54.480
And where are the issues?

00:14:54.480 --> 00:14:56.692
What are the challenges
that you are facing?

00:14:56.692 --> 00:14:58.275
And how do we provide
that information

00:14:58.275 --> 00:15:02.600
using the registered channels
or within the product as well?

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:04.809
Now we sell-- you
guys all now Cisco

00:15:04.809 --> 00:15:06.600
has so many products
and so many softwares,

00:15:06.600 --> 00:15:10.890
it's just not possible
humanly to do all of that.

00:15:10.890 --> 00:15:12.630
So we had to apply them--

00:15:12.630 --> 00:15:14.400
we had to use the machine.

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:16.740
To start with, we had to
go with business rules.

00:15:16.740 --> 00:15:21.360
But later on, we went into
AI and all those things.

00:15:21.360 --> 00:15:23.220
From a technology
point of view, I still

00:15:23.220 --> 00:15:26.790
feel that there
is a lot of scope

00:15:26.790 --> 00:15:28.890
in technology improvement.

00:15:28.890 --> 00:15:32.000
For example, when we
look at AI, AI today

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:33.530
is a very specialized AI.

00:15:33.530 --> 00:15:34.980
It can do certain things.

00:15:34.980 --> 00:15:38.520
But when you look at generic
AI, it's still not there yet.

00:15:38.520 --> 00:15:40.890
And I don't think the
technology is even there to.

00:15:40.890 --> 00:15:46.180
But we cannot just think about
technology as a roadblock,

00:15:46.180 --> 00:15:49.949
but think about what can
we do, what exists today?

00:15:49.949 --> 00:15:51.490
And what is it that
we want to drive?

00:15:51.490 --> 00:15:54.300
So when we started is journey,
it was more about can we

00:15:54.300 --> 00:15:57.660
know our customers better, like
customer segmentation, customer

00:15:57.660 --> 00:16:00.080
journey, how do they
use the product?

00:16:00.080 --> 00:16:01.800
What are the features
that they use?

00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:03.340
What they want to achieve?

00:16:03.340 --> 00:16:05.610
What should we tell
them to use them more?

00:16:05.610 --> 00:16:07.420
And through all
digital channels,

00:16:07.420 --> 00:16:09.660
how we can communicate
that information back

00:16:09.660 --> 00:16:12.880
to the customer, and then see
what works, what doesn't work?

00:16:12.880 --> 00:16:16.380
So this things took
a lot of effort,

00:16:16.380 --> 00:16:18.240
but this is where we
are heading towards.

00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:22.590
And we are working tirelessly
to meet these goals.

00:16:22.590 --> 00:16:24.690
SALIL AMONKAR: Very good.

00:16:24.690 --> 00:16:27.730
You mentioned a very good
thing, you mentioned journey.

00:16:27.730 --> 00:16:31.200
So Sudhakar, if I may ask
you, he gave the perspective

00:16:31.200 --> 00:16:34.380
from the digital marketing.

00:16:34.380 --> 00:16:37.410
What's your perspective
on this AI/ML journey

00:16:37.410 --> 00:16:41.725
as it relates to supply
planning, supply operations?

00:16:41.725 --> 00:16:45.000
SUDHAKAR PARAKALA: So
the space where Synaptics

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:47.550
is in, it's in a
high-tech world,

00:16:47.550 --> 00:16:51.150
fast-moving products, and
cell phones, and tablets,

00:16:51.150 --> 00:16:52.080
and laptops.

00:16:52.080 --> 00:16:56.760
And the market that we are on
are the Alexas in the world,

00:16:56.760 --> 00:16:59.340
so our time to market.

00:16:59.340 --> 00:17:03.330
And we have to make sure that
our inventory is optimized.

00:17:03.330 --> 00:17:05.880
At the same time, you don't
want to leave any money

00:17:05.880 --> 00:17:06.510
on the table.

00:17:06.510 --> 00:17:12.750
So our use case was, hey, if you
have to optimize the inventory,

00:17:12.750 --> 00:17:15.270
then how do we go
about it, then we are--

00:17:15.270 --> 00:17:17.310
absolutely we wanted
to focus on the demand.

00:17:17.310 --> 00:17:21.630
So though our goal was inventing
optimization, so we thought,

00:17:21.630 --> 00:17:23.750
why not start
looking into demand?

00:17:23.750 --> 00:17:25.530
There's a lot of fluctuation.

00:17:25.530 --> 00:17:27.690
There's a lot of inputs
coming from different parts

00:17:27.690 --> 00:17:28.770
of the world.

00:17:28.770 --> 00:17:31.090
And things are changing
as you can see,

00:17:31.090 --> 00:17:33.210
I think the cell phone--

00:17:33.210 --> 00:17:36.187
the life of a cell
phone is pretty small

00:17:36.187 --> 00:17:38.020
when compared to some
of the other products.

00:17:38.020 --> 00:17:42.180
So we had to look
into areas where

00:17:42.180 --> 00:17:44.220
we could look into demand.

00:17:44.220 --> 00:17:48.477
How would we try to look into
demand, predictive analytics

00:17:48.477 --> 00:17:49.060
kind of thing.

00:17:49.060 --> 00:17:50.820
So that's when we started.

00:17:50.820 --> 00:17:54.160
Why not start looking into ML?

00:17:54.160 --> 00:17:56.910
And so our journey has been,
we thought, let's start

00:17:56.910 --> 00:17:59.970
looking into, if we can,
have ML predict our forecast

00:17:59.970 --> 00:18:02.250
for the stable mature product.

00:18:02.250 --> 00:18:06.490
So obviously, the goal was to
do the inventory optimization.

00:18:06.490 --> 00:18:08.070
So, it's been a great journey.

00:18:08.070 --> 00:18:12.650
We did get to a point wherein
for certain mature products,

00:18:12.650 --> 00:18:13.830
we do have--

00:18:13.830 --> 00:18:16.620
getting some confidence
that maybe we

00:18:16.620 --> 00:18:20.850
can start leveraging ML to
start predicting our forecast,

00:18:20.850 --> 00:18:24.650
as you mentioned before, so we
can focus on the exceptions.

00:18:24.650 --> 00:18:28.070
So standard products,
everything that's mature, maybe

00:18:28.070 --> 00:18:31.070
ML will drive the demand,
and the rest of it we

00:18:31.070 --> 00:18:33.880
manage by exception.

00:18:33.880 --> 00:18:36.182
So it's been a great
journey so far.

00:18:36.182 --> 00:18:38.650
SALIL AMONKAR: Taking
from that, and I may--

00:18:38.650 --> 00:18:44.350
Adam if I may ask you to add
to what Sudhakar just said.

00:18:44.350 --> 00:18:48.250
But you did a phenomenal--

00:18:48.250 --> 00:18:51.210
I mean your company
did a phenomenal work

00:18:51.210 --> 00:18:57.040
in applying AI and ML working
with us to really come up--

00:18:57.040 --> 00:19:00.140
I mean, do a change that
was-- it sound simple,

00:19:00.140 --> 00:19:03.220
what you presented in
great detail yesterday.

00:19:03.220 --> 00:19:05.100
What are your thoughts
that you would

00:19:05.100 --> 00:19:08.850
like to share with this audience
in allowing the AI and ML

00:19:08.850 --> 00:19:13.500
journey for innovation and
breakthrough business benefits?

00:19:13.500 --> 00:19:16.220
Taking that as an example
so that they can understand,

00:19:16.220 --> 00:19:18.980
for somebody who wants to
embark on that journey,

00:19:18.980 --> 00:19:22.777
what are the things that
one to pay attention to?

00:19:22.777 --> 00:19:23.610
ADAM SPUNBERG: Sure.

00:19:23.610 --> 00:19:27.600
And I think AB InBev
stands out a little bit

00:19:27.600 --> 00:19:29.790
from the other
companies on this panel

00:19:29.790 --> 00:19:31.860
because the work
that we're doing

00:19:31.860 --> 00:19:35.460
is not so much software-based.

00:19:35.460 --> 00:19:38.510
We're a manufacturing
company more or less.

00:19:38.510 --> 00:19:40.170
We're creating beer.

00:19:40.170 --> 00:19:42.660
And so you probably
wonder where does

00:19:42.660 --> 00:19:45.390
machine learning and artificial
intelligence play in there?

00:19:45.390 --> 00:19:48.430
Well actually, in
very many places,

00:19:48.430 --> 00:19:50.740
and I think we're just
scratching the surface.

00:19:50.740 --> 00:19:52.560
So to go into the
specific example

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:56.430
where Pluto7 is
working with us is we

00:19:56.430 --> 00:20:00.570
found that we could predict a
much more accurate filtration

00:20:00.570 --> 00:20:05.610
process by using
artificial intelligence.

00:20:05.610 --> 00:20:09.540
It's pretty remarkable how you
can study certain variables

00:20:09.540 --> 00:20:11.220
by going through
the data, and having

00:20:11.220 --> 00:20:14.580
enough data to do that,
and be able to predict

00:20:14.580 --> 00:20:17.280
with much keener
accuracy when you

00:20:17.280 --> 00:20:20.700
should replace a filter in
that fermentation process.

00:20:20.700 --> 00:20:23.280
So it's a pretty
extraordinary thing.

00:20:23.280 --> 00:20:27.360
And there's so much that goes
into making beer, for example,

00:20:27.360 --> 00:20:31.380
that this is just one example
that we're starting with

00:20:31.380 --> 00:20:34.240
to see how effective AI can be.

00:20:34.240 --> 00:20:37.060
And we're very optimistic
about those results.

00:20:37.060 --> 00:20:42.197
So I think spawning off from
that, any part of maintenance

00:20:42.197 --> 00:20:43.530
could be predictive maintenance.

00:20:43.530 --> 00:20:45.940
It could be something
in our packaging lines.

00:20:45.940 --> 00:20:49.460
It could be something
with maybe more along what

00:20:49.460 --> 00:20:53.880
you are suggesting, predicting
inventory or customer demand.

00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:56.910
There's really-- the
sky's the limit on this.

00:20:56.910 --> 00:21:00.570
And I think for all
of you out there

00:21:00.570 --> 00:21:03.360
that are involved in companies
who are considering this,

00:21:03.360 --> 00:21:06.120
this really does seem
to be the most cutting

00:21:06.120 --> 00:21:10.500
edge way to make that next
huge bump in advancement

00:21:10.500 --> 00:21:12.012
toward the future.

00:21:12.012 --> 00:21:13.470
SALIL AMONKAR: Thank you.

00:21:13.470 --> 00:21:15.540
And Deepak, if I
may ask you, you

00:21:15.540 --> 00:21:17.850
are a fast-growing retailer.

00:21:17.850 --> 00:21:21.480
You have a lot of
business growth

00:21:21.480 --> 00:21:24.090
and your scaling is
something that is always

00:21:24.090 --> 00:21:25.690
at the top of your mind.

00:21:25.690 --> 00:21:28.170
So with that in mind
and related to what

00:21:28.170 --> 00:21:30.005
Adam and Sudhakar
just discussed,

00:21:30.005 --> 00:21:31.005
what's your perspective?

00:21:31.005 --> 00:21:34.260
DEEPAK MEHROTRA: Yeah, so for
us at California Design Den,

00:21:34.260 --> 00:21:37.530
our focus has also
been trying to tie

00:21:37.530 --> 00:21:42.330
the two ends, which is demand
forecasting and supply chain.

00:21:42.330 --> 00:21:46.020
The whole question of
optimization of supply chain,

00:21:46.020 --> 00:21:52.020
being like a startup again with
limited resources and growth

00:21:52.020 --> 00:21:54.960
that's surpassing
and clearing anything

00:21:54.960 --> 00:21:59.370
that we had ever forecasted,
it became very important

00:21:59.370 --> 00:22:04.320
for us to ensure that the
decisions are near real time

00:22:04.320 --> 00:22:07.970
and, again, managing
by exception.

00:22:07.970 --> 00:22:10.860
Now imagine, I mean,
how many of you really

00:22:10.860 --> 00:22:12.600
think that a bed sheet--

00:22:12.600 --> 00:22:15.360
that's one of the key
products we make--

00:22:15.360 --> 00:22:19.165
needs a retail shelf space on
the seventh floor at Macy's?

00:22:19.165 --> 00:22:21.540
I mean, really, do we really
need a huge floor of selling

00:22:21.540 --> 00:22:22.350
bed Sheets?

00:22:22.350 --> 00:22:24.840
How many of you would go there
and be able to actually open

00:22:24.840 --> 00:22:26.760
one of those bags and feel it?

00:22:26.760 --> 00:22:27.720
Probably not.

00:22:27.720 --> 00:22:32.764
So we see products like ours,
which are fairly commoditized,

00:22:32.764 --> 00:22:34.650
completely being online.

00:22:34.650 --> 00:22:36.960
And therefore, the
growth is phenomenal.

00:22:36.960 --> 00:22:40.000
And we are competing
with our channels,

00:22:40.000 --> 00:22:42.780
whether it's Walmart.com
or Amazon.com,

00:22:42.780 --> 00:22:48.780
so it becomes very important for
us to be very nimble, be fast.

00:22:48.780 --> 00:22:51.582
And in the journey towards--

00:22:51.582 --> 00:22:55.320
from basically ensuring all
our data is in one place,

00:22:55.320 --> 00:22:57.870
everything is flowing
into one place,

00:22:57.870 --> 00:23:01.350
having a very distributed
supply chain, running from here

00:23:01.350 --> 00:23:05.040
to factories in
India and China, we

00:23:05.040 --> 00:23:09.120
have been taking on this journey
and seeing very good results.

00:23:09.120 --> 00:23:14.250
Of course, those results
are still very basic,

00:23:14.250 --> 00:23:18.810
whether it's inventory
reduction, or its speed

00:23:18.810 --> 00:23:22.800
in making decisions for changing
what is in production line.

00:23:22.800 --> 00:23:27.450
But how we are looking at ML and
then AI is that as we scale up,

00:23:27.450 --> 00:23:31.500
and we face this
hyper competition,

00:23:31.500 --> 00:23:34.890
we would be ready for whether
it's demand, forecasting,

00:23:34.890 --> 00:23:37.830
supply chain planning,
price optimization, and what

00:23:37.830 --> 00:23:39.285
have you.

00:23:39.285 --> 00:23:40.770
SALIL AMONKAR: Thank you.

00:23:40.770 --> 00:23:44.760
So Vikram, coming back
to you and marketing

00:23:44.760 --> 00:23:46.690
on a similar theme.

00:23:46.690 --> 00:23:50.190
Now that you've considered--
you've looked at--

00:23:50.190 --> 00:23:51.980
I think you always
drove development

00:23:51.980 --> 00:23:54.510
when it came to technology
because you have to do

00:23:54.510 --> 00:23:57.160
that in digital marketing.

00:23:57.160 --> 00:24:00.090
What are the considerations
especially around going

00:24:00.090 --> 00:24:02.940
about your AI and
ML journey that you

00:24:02.940 --> 00:24:08.370
want to point out, that would
be of interest to this audience?

00:24:08.370 --> 00:24:10.330
VIKRAM VERMA: Is it
about strategical--

00:24:10.330 --> 00:24:11.520
SALIL AMONKAR: Tactical.

00:24:11.520 --> 00:24:13.314
Let's talk about both.

00:24:13.314 --> 00:24:14.730
VIKRAM VERMA: So
one of the things

00:24:14.730 --> 00:24:18.290
that I would say,
we have been using

00:24:18.290 --> 00:24:20.345
a lot of prediction-based
modeling.

00:24:20.345 --> 00:24:22.200
Earlier we used to
call it as forecasting

00:24:22.200 --> 00:24:26.490
for late '90s in Cisco from
a supply chain point of view.

00:24:26.490 --> 00:24:29.830
But this motion of
using the data science

00:24:29.830 --> 00:24:34.386
not from forecasting, but from
prediction point is pretty new.

00:24:34.386 --> 00:24:35.760
And from strategical
angle, there

00:24:35.760 --> 00:24:38.760
are multiple things which
comes top of the mind.

00:24:38.760 --> 00:24:41.822
There is a big hype
around AI and ML.

00:24:41.822 --> 00:24:44.400
A lot of companies
today are investing

00:24:44.400 --> 00:24:47.100
because they feel
it's magic, and it's

00:24:47.100 --> 00:24:50.670
one silver bullet which will
solve all of their problems.

00:24:50.670 --> 00:24:52.740
But I would just caution it.

00:24:52.740 --> 00:24:53.890
It's not a magic--

00:24:53.890 --> 00:24:57.320
it's not one silver bullet
which will solve your problem.

00:24:57.320 --> 00:24:59.580
it's a big step forward.

00:24:59.580 --> 00:25:02.710
And it requires a lot of hard
process change in the company

00:25:02.710 --> 00:25:04.410
itself.

00:25:04.410 --> 00:25:08.110
Your business objective
should be very, very clear.

00:25:08.110 --> 00:25:15.040
A second thing is the way
you use it from a technology

00:25:15.040 --> 00:25:17.110
and from data point of
view, data science and AI

00:25:17.110 --> 00:25:19.830
can only work on
the data itself.

00:25:19.830 --> 00:25:22.140
If you don't have the
data in a way that

00:25:22.140 --> 00:25:24.750
could be used for
machine, it doesn't

00:25:24.750 --> 00:25:28.150
mean that you need to have
Amazon or Google like exabytes

00:25:28.150 --> 00:25:29.790
or petabytes kind of data.

00:25:29.790 --> 00:25:31.680
But you should have
enough diversified data

00:25:31.680 --> 00:25:34.950
that you could use it for
machine learning algorithm.

00:25:34.950 --> 00:25:38.140
And the third thing is, as I
said, it's not a silver bullet.

00:25:38.140 --> 00:25:41.040
It requires a lot of
work, sharing the data in.

00:25:41.040 --> 00:25:42.870
And there should
be a willingness

00:25:42.870 --> 00:25:48.940
to take that insight from the ML
and put them into the process.

00:25:48.940 --> 00:25:50.684
Sometimes we get
a lot of insights,

00:25:50.684 --> 00:25:52.850
but when we are trying to
drive that experience with

00:25:52.850 --> 00:25:55.510
the customers, like
Cisco.com, for example,

00:25:55.510 --> 00:25:58.290
when we want to say this
customer wants to only learn

00:25:58.290 --> 00:26:00.450
about this, tell
him that message,

00:26:00.450 --> 00:26:03.660
it goes into that channel,
oh, we know more--

00:26:03.660 --> 00:26:06.650
should this be the
message, or no,

00:26:06.650 --> 00:26:09.550
this page cannot be
customized for an example.

00:26:09.550 --> 00:26:12.510
So sometimes it's in the
front end kind of work,

00:26:12.510 --> 00:26:15.630
it becomes a difficult
when you the insight,

00:26:15.630 --> 00:26:17.220
you know what the
customer wants.

00:26:17.220 --> 00:26:19.410
But when you want to show
that message to customer,

00:26:19.410 --> 00:26:21.504
your tools platform
doesn't support.

00:26:21.504 --> 00:26:22.920
So there are a lot
of constraints.

00:26:22.920 --> 00:26:25.211
I will just caution that
first from a strategical point

00:26:25.211 --> 00:26:31.380
of view that AI is not which
can be implemented tomorrow.

00:26:31.380 --> 00:26:36.795
It takes some time, especially
in a big company like Cisco.

00:26:36.795 --> 00:26:38.670
The second thing from
technical point of view

00:26:38.670 --> 00:26:42.890
is normal activities, like
what is it, till the time--

00:26:42.890 --> 00:26:44.265
if you had a
business objects are

00:26:44.265 --> 00:26:47.460
clear till the
time you get there,

00:26:47.460 --> 00:26:49.740
how can you keep on adding
the value in the process?

00:26:49.740 --> 00:26:52.770
Because everybody
thinks about AI,

00:26:52.770 --> 00:26:55.540
they always think about either
optimization, whether it

00:26:55.540 --> 00:26:57.440
you say cost or process.

00:26:57.440 --> 00:27:00.350
Or you say, how can
I get the innovation

00:27:00.350 --> 00:27:02.280
or competitive advantage?

00:27:02.280 --> 00:27:05.150
And how can I use it to extend
our business to the next level?

00:27:05.150 --> 00:27:07.650
But all of these things, when
you go to the technical level,

00:27:07.650 --> 00:27:08.970
these things will take time.

00:27:08.970 --> 00:27:11.602
And technically, you cannot
wait for a couple of years

00:27:11.602 --> 00:27:12.560
to produce the results.

00:27:12.560 --> 00:27:15.520
So you have to understand what
those business challenges are,

00:27:15.520 --> 00:27:19.000
where the things could
immediately add the value.

00:27:19.000 --> 00:27:20.520
So for example, we
were lucky enough

00:27:20.520 --> 00:27:23.370
that we were part of
a sales organization.

00:27:23.370 --> 00:27:25.140
And there are a lot
of companies which

00:27:25.140 --> 00:27:27.180
has done a lot of
things for the sales

00:27:27.180 --> 00:27:30.210
already, so we had to take those
learnings and see it just to

00:27:30.210 --> 00:27:31.570
of our own culture.

00:27:31.570 --> 00:27:35.580
So for example, customer churn
model, customer risk analysis,

00:27:35.580 --> 00:27:38.760
lifecycle advantage,
product recommendation,

00:27:38.760 --> 00:27:42.690
all those things what is the
right contract on this company?

00:27:42.690 --> 00:27:44.940
What is the timing that
you should call that guy?

00:27:44.940 --> 00:27:46.410
What is that
pre-scripted insight

00:27:46.410 --> 00:27:48.360
when you talk to that customer?

00:27:48.360 --> 00:27:50.580
All of these things
came in handy

00:27:50.580 --> 00:27:52.530
to drive that
immediate value that we

00:27:52.530 --> 00:27:56.490
had to show step-by-step before
we get to that bigger goal.

00:27:56.490 --> 00:27:58.740
The second thing from a
customer success point of view

00:27:58.740 --> 00:28:02.250
was all about, how do we provide
the right relevant message

00:28:02.250 --> 00:28:06.010
for the customer and
change their experience?

00:28:06.010 --> 00:28:08.520
So technically,
its more around how

00:28:08.520 --> 00:28:10.350
can you keep on
adding the value,

00:28:10.350 --> 00:28:14.211
but only when your strategic
objectives are very clear.

00:28:14.211 --> 00:28:16.100
SALIL AMONKAR: Very
good points there.

00:28:16.100 --> 00:28:20.730
I heard keeping focus on data
which we heard even during some

00:28:20.730 --> 00:28:22.270
of the keynotes I heard.

00:28:22.270 --> 00:28:26.850
Rajen mentioned that yesterday
and then also people always

00:28:26.850 --> 00:28:30.600
vendors talk about AI and ML,
its all about data and then

00:28:30.600 --> 00:28:32.610
understanding where
you want to go.

00:28:32.610 --> 00:28:36.600
So Sudhakar similarly,
any insights

00:28:36.600 --> 00:28:40.830
you want to talk about in the
first ML/AI proof of concept

00:28:40.830 --> 00:28:44.774
that you did that you
would like to point out?

00:28:44.774 --> 00:28:47.190
SUDHAKAR PARAKALA: I think it
has to be tightly integrated

00:28:47.190 --> 00:28:48.970
with business planning.

00:28:48.970 --> 00:28:54.780
So it's not just about
having data and trying

00:28:54.780 --> 00:28:58.230
to come up with a magic formula
that its going to predict.

00:28:58.230 --> 00:29:01.680
Especially for our use case,
it was demand plan, come up

00:29:01.680 --> 00:29:03.790
with an accurate demand plan.

00:29:03.790 --> 00:29:05.839
So you have to be
tightly integrated

00:29:05.839 --> 00:29:08.130
with business planning as to
what are the elements that

00:29:08.130 --> 00:29:09.790
go into a business plan.

00:29:09.790 --> 00:29:13.200
One has to work with
the given amount of data

00:29:13.200 --> 00:29:16.500
that is available to
put those things along

00:29:16.500 --> 00:29:20.100
with the algorithms that
the ML can come up with.

00:29:20.100 --> 00:29:22.410
So it's not just
throwing in data and then

00:29:22.410 --> 00:29:24.027
its going to spit
out an end result,

00:29:24.027 --> 00:29:25.860
and that's going to be
an accurate forecast.

00:29:25.860 --> 00:29:28.410
That's not happening.

00:29:28.410 --> 00:29:30.000
So the business plan--

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:33.030
anything that subjective,
objective, or anything

00:29:33.030 --> 00:29:35.310
that you're getting from
the field data, when

00:29:35.310 --> 00:29:40.200
one is creating that demand
plan and the business plan,

00:29:40.200 --> 00:29:43.980
any element that goes
into the thought process,

00:29:43.980 --> 00:29:48.390
we have to make sure
that the ML can--

00:29:48.390 --> 00:29:51.930
we could try to incorporate
as much as you can,

00:29:51.930 --> 00:29:54.990
only then maybe it
starts to understanding

00:29:54.990 --> 00:29:57.030
that this not one
thing that you look

00:29:57.030 --> 00:29:58.990
into when you enter a demand.

00:29:58.990 --> 00:30:02.490
So if you're the person that's
responsible for next year,

00:30:02.490 --> 00:30:05.070
maybe or whatever,
how many folks

00:30:05.070 --> 00:30:08.310
are going to attend
this conference,

00:30:08.310 --> 00:30:11.040
so it could not be
one factor or two

00:30:11.040 --> 00:30:13.200
for you to accurately
predict that.

00:30:13.200 --> 00:30:15.710
Similarly, we had
to get into that--

00:30:15.710 --> 00:30:17.370
it has to be very
tightly integrated

00:30:17.370 --> 00:30:19.500
with the overall business plan.

00:30:19.500 --> 00:30:21.420
And it meant, like
you said, it's

00:30:21.420 --> 00:30:24.390
not something that
comes out automatically,

00:30:24.390 --> 00:30:27.420
so you have to then look
into what products can you

00:30:27.420 --> 00:30:29.250
really apply that.

00:30:29.250 --> 00:30:30.880
And that's going
to be more helpful.

00:30:30.880 --> 00:30:33.390
So we're still in
a very early stage,

00:30:33.390 --> 00:30:35.160
but we do see some
positive results.

00:30:35.160 --> 00:30:37.560
Of course, some places
there are disappointments,

00:30:37.560 --> 00:30:41.340
so we just have to continue
to tweak it, and fine tune

00:30:41.340 --> 00:30:42.700
it, and get to a point.

00:30:42.700 --> 00:30:47.310
So it's extremely
important to put

00:30:47.310 --> 00:30:51.460
all the elements that are there
in our business plan into it.

00:30:51.460 --> 00:30:54.960
It's been a great
journey as to--

00:30:54.960 --> 00:30:57.160
we had some successes
and some, obviously,

00:30:57.160 --> 00:30:58.646
we still have to learn.

00:30:58.646 --> 00:31:02.940
SALIL AMONKAR: So Adam, I
heard from Deepak that it's not

00:31:02.940 --> 00:31:04.900
magic, it requires work.

00:31:04.900 --> 00:31:06.900
And Sudhakar mentioned
that hey, there has

00:31:06.900 --> 00:31:11.030
to be focus and a plan behind.

00:31:11.030 --> 00:31:14.150
In your example, one of
the things that I heard--

00:31:14.150 --> 00:31:16.940
and I want to relate
it to your experience--

00:31:16.940 --> 00:31:18.646
that was president
in the keynote

00:31:18.646 --> 00:31:22.160
today by Rajen Sheth is
it's all about simple AI.

00:31:22.160 --> 00:31:24.230
And I think in your
case, what we achieved,

00:31:24.230 --> 00:31:27.560
or what you achieved was
something that at the end

00:31:27.560 --> 00:31:29.600
became very simple.

00:31:29.600 --> 00:31:33.076
So any thoughts around that?

00:31:33.076 --> 00:31:35.300
ADAM SPUNBERG: Well,
one thing I think

00:31:35.300 --> 00:31:38.180
is interesting about what
we're all trying to do here,

00:31:38.180 --> 00:31:42.300
and I'll use our case with
AB InBev as an example,

00:31:42.300 --> 00:31:45.000
is we're talking so much
about machine learning,

00:31:45.000 --> 00:31:48.050
but what are we really
trying to relate to?

00:31:48.050 --> 00:31:49.880
That's humans.

00:31:49.880 --> 00:31:53.480
So it's an irony in a way
that we're using a machine

00:31:53.480 --> 00:31:56.090
to better understand ourselves.

00:31:56.090 --> 00:32:00.680
So until we enter a
world where maybe robots

00:32:00.680 --> 00:32:03.050
are running everything and
then they'd be marketing

00:32:03.050 --> 00:32:04.310
to themselves, who knows.

00:32:04.310 --> 00:32:07.070
But for now, it's all
about that human aspect.

00:32:07.070 --> 00:32:10.910
And that actually doesn't
just go for consumers.

00:32:10.910 --> 00:32:14.090
That's also within the
organization itself.

00:32:14.090 --> 00:32:17.570
And that's a battle
that I've had to fight.

00:32:17.570 --> 00:32:20.270
And I don't say that
is a negative thing.

00:32:20.270 --> 00:32:22.880
I think it's about
educating and getting

00:32:22.880 --> 00:32:25.190
more and more people
aligned, especially

00:32:25.190 --> 00:32:26.510
a big company like ours.

00:32:26.510 --> 00:32:31.220
AB InBev has 250,000
employees or some such.

00:32:31.220 --> 00:32:35.470
So you're not going to have
250,000 people on board

00:32:35.470 --> 00:32:39.440
with AI from day one, and
certainly not key stakeholders

00:32:39.440 --> 00:32:41.900
who aren't familiar with
the technologies yet

00:32:41.900 --> 00:32:44.570
and haven't had a chance to
really understand the value

00:32:44.570 --> 00:32:46.040
that it can bring.

00:32:46.040 --> 00:32:50.450
So I think you say simple
AI, but what I would--

00:32:50.450 --> 00:32:54.650
taking a play on those words,
I think a real key for us

00:32:54.650 --> 00:32:57.770
if we want to lead this AI
revolution is to find a way

00:32:57.770 --> 00:32:59.900
to simplify the message.

00:32:59.900 --> 00:33:06.110
How can we show this value to
everybody, top up and down,

00:33:06.110 --> 00:33:09.020
that they can see
what we can achieve?

00:33:09.020 --> 00:33:10.760
And that it's not a threat.

00:33:10.760 --> 00:33:13.421
If anything, it's
an enhancement.

00:33:13.421 --> 00:33:15.090
SALIL AMONKAR: Thank you.

00:33:15.090 --> 00:33:15.965
Deepak, any comments?

00:33:18.890 --> 00:33:22.580
DEEPAK MEHROTRA: I think as
far as ML and AI is concerned,

00:33:22.580 --> 00:33:24.230
it's more like a journey.

00:33:24.230 --> 00:33:26.270
So as you said, it's
not a magic bullet.

00:33:26.270 --> 00:33:31.330
And the biggest advantage that
we feel at California Design

00:33:31.330 --> 00:33:37.160
Den, trying to ensure that
we set the vision that this

00:33:37.160 --> 00:33:39.900
is what is going to drive
competitive advantage, then

00:33:39.900 --> 00:33:44.000
product differentiation may not
be a differentiating factor.

00:33:44.000 --> 00:33:46.040
This kind of drives
us as a company

00:33:46.040 --> 00:33:47.730
to set everything in place.

00:33:47.730 --> 00:33:49.910
So it's not that
we have to set up

00:33:49.910 --> 00:33:52.880
some new system that's only
going to do ML-based demand

00:33:52.880 --> 00:33:54.020
forecasting.

00:33:54.020 --> 00:33:57.550
So whether it is basic
analytics to advance analytics

00:33:57.550 --> 00:34:02.480
or what have you,
it's like a journey.

00:34:02.480 --> 00:34:06.390
But once you have a vision that
this is where you need to be,

00:34:06.390 --> 00:34:10.790
and many of those visions
are still visionary visions,

00:34:10.790 --> 00:34:12.679
this does bring in a
lot of efficiencies.

00:34:12.679 --> 00:34:15.590
It becomes a rallying point
between the key stakeholders.

00:34:18.989 --> 00:34:20.630
There's an added
advantage where you'll

00:34:20.630 --> 00:34:22.250
have to relook at
all your processes

00:34:22.250 --> 00:34:24.860
and what have you
because as Adam said,

00:34:24.860 --> 00:34:27.090
not everyone gets
on board right away.

00:34:27.090 --> 00:34:29.989
So you internally
debate, figure out

00:34:29.989 --> 00:34:35.190
what do you do, break
all molds, make new ones.

00:34:35.190 --> 00:34:39.440
So we see AI/ML
not just in what we

00:34:39.440 --> 00:34:42.080
expect it to produce
after a few years,

00:34:42.080 --> 00:34:45.570
but also as a driving force
towards being on your feet

00:34:45.570 --> 00:34:49.446
and just pushing hard,
and being in the front.

00:34:49.446 --> 00:34:52.710
SALIL AMONKAR:
Thank you, Deepak.

00:34:52.710 --> 00:34:56.540
So now between Vikram,
you and Sudhakar,

00:34:56.540 --> 00:35:00.620
your represent the
high tech industry.

00:35:00.620 --> 00:35:02.300
I would like to hear
Vikram first you

00:35:02.300 --> 00:35:06.140
and then Sudhakar following
him, your thoughts on--

00:35:06.140 --> 00:35:09.850
now that you have
embarked on this AI and ML

00:35:09.850 --> 00:35:12.320
and started using it,
at least beginning

00:35:12.320 --> 00:35:15.847
to understand how to use it
in your respective spaces,

00:35:15.847 --> 00:35:16.430
I would like--

00:35:16.430 --> 00:35:20.182
I think you articulated,
although in some places,

00:35:20.182 --> 00:35:26.240
that it's a journey, what do
you see in the high tech space?

00:35:26.240 --> 00:35:29.360
Where do you see AI
being leveraged more?

00:35:29.360 --> 00:35:31.940
And where do you want
to give this audience--

00:35:31.940 --> 00:35:35.355
OK, here you might be
tempted to go into AI,

00:35:35.355 --> 00:35:39.209
but you may want to go with
a little bit of caution.

00:35:39.209 --> 00:35:41.000
Because I think one of
the things that what

00:35:41.000 --> 00:35:43.507
I'm trying to understand
is in your experience

00:35:43.507 --> 00:35:45.840
that I'm sure what the audience
would like to understand

00:35:45.840 --> 00:35:48.530
is, as you are going
through these journeys,

00:35:48.530 --> 00:35:50.180
there are learnings.

00:35:50.180 --> 00:35:55.340
OK, AI, everybody wants to
get into the AI/ML bandwagon.

00:35:55.340 --> 00:35:58.830
But not every use case may
be meant for AI and ML.

00:35:58.830 --> 00:36:01.940
So any perspective taking
that into consideration?

00:36:01.940 --> 00:36:03.200
VIKRAM VERMA: Yes.

00:36:03.200 --> 00:36:05.150
When we talk about
AI or ML, there

00:36:05.150 --> 00:36:08.270
are always risks and
benefits to that as well.

00:36:08.270 --> 00:36:10.960
When we talk about any
model or any recommendation,

00:36:10.960 --> 00:36:14.240
there is always a
percentage of accuracy.

00:36:14.240 --> 00:36:16.240
And one of the things
that I was talking earlier

00:36:16.240 --> 00:36:18.210
was when we look
at the MLs today,

00:36:18.210 --> 00:36:20.860
these are specialized
models, specialized models

00:36:20.860 --> 00:36:24.340
learning from the data
about previous behavior.

00:36:24.340 --> 00:36:25.970
With these set of
behaviors, this

00:36:25.970 --> 00:36:28.000
was the outcome
that [INAUDIBLE]..

00:36:28.000 --> 00:36:30.190
And that drives your
prediction about what shall

00:36:30.190 --> 00:36:32.860
you do tomorrow because
there's the same pattern, which

00:36:32.860 --> 00:36:35.530
will repeat the same
thing, assuming that.

00:36:35.530 --> 00:36:40.240
So when we put the models in, we
get one thing is we cannot just

00:36:40.240 --> 00:36:41.410
blindly trust the model.

00:36:41.410 --> 00:36:45.460
There has to be some way of
understanding what those models

00:36:45.460 --> 00:36:46.930
or outputs are talking about.

00:36:46.930 --> 00:36:49.660
Put the human intelligence
around it, and then

00:36:49.660 --> 00:36:54.290
say, does this case
even make sense or not?

00:36:54.290 --> 00:36:56.230
So when we were
working on a project,

00:36:56.230 --> 00:36:59.080
customer segmentation-- which we
are still working on to evolve

00:36:59.080 --> 00:37:01.070
it to the next level--

00:37:01.070 --> 00:37:06.340
we realized that we were able to
divide our customers in around,

00:37:06.340 --> 00:37:10.090
for that one particular product,
the customer set into like 16

00:37:10.090 --> 00:37:11.560
different segments.

00:37:11.560 --> 00:37:14.560
But when we looked at with
the business owners trying

00:37:14.560 --> 00:37:17.260
to understand what does
it really represent,

00:37:17.260 --> 00:37:19.360
and how shall we drive
that experience of each

00:37:19.360 --> 00:37:22.930
of the segments, what each
of the set of customers, half

00:37:22.930 --> 00:37:25.966
of them did not even make sense
to them because it was more

00:37:25.966 --> 00:37:27.340
around, you know
what, this seems

00:37:27.340 --> 00:37:28.506
pretty much similar to that.

00:37:28.506 --> 00:37:30.590
Their messaging
will be different.

00:37:30.590 --> 00:37:32.260
So why don't we
merge it together?

00:37:32.260 --> 00:37:35.170
This one does not even make
sense because these things is

00:37:35.170 --> 00:37:37.810
like, if everyone is
using perfectly, then

00:37:37.810 --> 00:37:39.770
what will we do with them?

00:37:39.770 --> 00:37:43.900
So when we look at the
machine, these kind of risk,

00:37:43.900 --> 00:37:46.390
we cannot blindly
trust the machine yet.

00:37:46.390 --> 00:37:48.498
It's a specialized
AI, which does

00:37:48.498 --> 00:37:52.570
specialized things even better
than human in certain aspect.

00:37:52.570 --> 00:37:56.080
But human supervision in putting
that through the motion is

00:37:56.080 --> 00:37:59.780
still very much required,
that intuition of looking--

00:37:59.780 --> 00:38:04.550
let's say these patterns
meant customers will order,

00:38:04.550 --> 00:38:08.350
but those patterns does not mean
much in today's environment.

00:38:08.350 --> 00:38:10.630
How can we understand from it?

00:38:10.630 --> 00:38:13.430
How can we apply
our human knowledge?

00:38:13.430 --> 00:38:16.660
And how can we keep
it always tuned

00:38:16.660 --> 00:38:20.770
in to the realistic business
scenario becomes the most

00:38:20.770 --> 00:38:22.000
important work in the AI?

00:38:22.000 --> 00:38:26.020
It's not just getting the
data, getting meaningful data,

00:38:26.020 --> 00:38:28.990
deriving the insight, and
putting the insight to work,

00:38:28.990 --> 00:38:30.910
but it's all about
taking that model,

00:38:30.910 --> 00:38:33.280
making an intelligent
decision around it,

00:38:33.280 --> 00:38:35.020
and then putting
it to work, which

00:38:35.020 --> 00:38:37.880
makes it a [INAUDIBLE] cycle.

00:38:37.880 --> 00:38:41.990
So human interaction is very
important, in my opinion,

00:38:41.990 --> 00:38:44.650
to take this AI to the market.

00:38:44.650 --> 00:38:47.500
Maybe when the technology
was in certain time,

00:38:47.500 --> 00:38:50.720
and when we have
general purpose AI,

00:38:50.720 --> 00:38:52.270
then it's a different story.

00:38:52.270 --> 00:38:54.610
But for now, it's
a specialized AI.

00:38:54.610 --> 00:38:59.040
And MLs are specialized
models based on the data.

00:38:59.040 --> 00:39:02.530
It should not be taken
on the face value.

00:39:02.530 --> 00:39:04.840
There are certain use
cases, like vision detection

00:39:04.840 --> 00:39:07.330
and all those things where
AI has already improved

00:39:07.330 --> 00:39:09.010
beyond human capability.

00:39:09.010 --> 00:39:11.710
But when we talk about business
sense or what makes sense

00:39:11.710 --> 00:39:16.030
and what does not, I think
putting blind trust in that

00:39:16.030 --> 00:39:20.680
and taking the action on
that without supervision can

00:39:20.680 --> 00:39:22.360
produce more--

00:39:22.360 --> 00:39:25.985
can produce more
risk than advantages

00:39:25.985 --> 00:39:26.860
in certain scenarios.

00:39:26.860 --> 00:39:27.820
SALIL AMONKAR: OK, thank you.

00:39:27.820 --> 00:39:29.320
I think the summary
of what you said

00:39:29.320 --> 00:39:33.580
is any AI solution requires
technology and domain

00:39:33.580 --> 00:39:35.020
and business expertise.

00:39:35.020 --> 00:39:37.210
And the second
thing is there are

00:39:37.210 --> 00:39:39.500
models that can be
leveraged as is,

00:39:39.500 --> 00:39:43.180
meaning what they call
the pre-built models,

00:39:43.180 --> 00:39:44.920
vision API you talked about.

00:39:44.920 --> 00:39:47.017
But there are certain
situations and some

00:39:47.017 --> 00:39:48.600
of the most of the
business situations

00:39:48.600 --> 00:39:50.183
where you have to
have a custom model.

00:39:50.183 --> 00:39:50.689
Thank you.

00:39:50.689 --> 00:39:52.022
Sudhakar, any perspective from--

00:39:52.022 --> 00:39:54.280
SUDHAKAR PARAKALA: So,
we took a little bit

00:39:54.280 --> 00:39:56.950
of a simplistic
approach, wherein

00:39:56.950 --> 00:40:00.310
obviously, like Adam
mentioned, first thing was ML

00:40:00.310 --> 00:40:01.960
is going to predict my demand?

00:40:01.960 --> 00:40:02.620
No way.

00:40:02.620 --> 00:40:06.624
So can ML really predict
what kind of dinner

00:40:06.624 --> 00:40:07.540
you are going to have?

00:40:07.540 --> 00:40:08.150
No way.

00:40:08.150 --> 00:40:11.579
So obviously there was
that kind of a thinking

00:40:11.579 --> 00:40:12.370
within the company.

00:40:12.370 --> 00:40:15.350
Then we took a much more
simplistic approach as to OK,

00:40:15.350 --> 00:40:19.780
in all the different product
lines or different customer

00:40:19.780 --> 00:40:22.660
segments, let's pick
something that where

00:40:22.660 --> 00:40:25.810
you think this could be a little
bit of more predictability

00:40:25.810 --> 00:40:27.550
within the human thinking.

00:40:27.550 --> 00:40:29.080
So within your own
thinking, there

00:40:29.080 --> 00:40:32.110
could be certain things
that you are more

00:40:32.110 --> 00:40:33.820
consistent in your thinking.

00:40:33.820 --> 00:40:35.400
There could be
some where you are

00:40:35.400 --> 00:40:38.980
trying to gather additional
data to digest and come up

00:40:38.980 --> 00:40:41.440
with a solution or an answer.

00:40:41.440 --> 00:40:45.700
So then we have to dig through
those questions as to OK,

00:40:45.700 --> 00:40:48.220
what are the areas
where you could

00:40:48.220 --> 00:40:49.840
be more consistent,
the customer could

00:40:49.840 --> 00:40:52.540
be more consistent, the market
could be more consistent,

00:40:52.540 --> 00:40:56.620
so we can drive our inventory
optimization by understanding

00:40:56.620 --> 00:40:58.640
how the demand is
being generated,

00:40:58.640 --> 00:41:04.630
so we picked up more of those
mature products our markets.

00:41:04.630 --> 00:41:07.970
So obviously, the acceptance
has to come into picture

00:41:07.970 --> 00:41:11.530
that hey, what the
machine is predicting

00:41:11.530 --> 00:41:13.930
is pretty much close to
what can I rely on it?

00:41:13.930 --> 00:41:17.020
So you have to take
the use case wherein

00:41:17.020 --> 00:41:20.230
one starts getting confidence
and so obviously, you

00:41:20.230 --> 00:41:22.900
want to pick up the products
and the markets that are

00:41:22.900 --> 00:41:25.210
a little bit more consistent.

00:41:25.210 --> 00:41:27.610
Obviously the value is
in the unpredictability

00:41:27.610 --> 00:41:30.050
where it can predict, but
you can't start with that.

00:41:30.050 --> 00:41:31.490
So we took a
simplistic approach.

00:41:31.490 --> 00:41:32.950
Let's take baby steps.

00:41:32.950 --> 00:41:35.630
So we are at that stage where
we came up with the first use

00:41:35.630 --> 00:41:37.940
case, now we'll take
it to the next level.

00:41:37.940 --> 00:41:41.650
Can I really apply it to the
NPI, new product introduction?

00:41:41.650 --> 00:41:43.240
We don't know.

00:41:43.240 --> 00:41:46.539
I think that's to be seen.

00:41:46.539 --> 00:41:47.580
SALIL AMONKAR: Thank you.

00:41:47.580 --> 00:41:52.100
I think the summary there is
crawl, walk, and then run.

00:41:52.100 --> 00:41:57.080
Adam, I think from an
AB InBev prospective,

00:41:57.080 --> 00:42:01.510
you presented a very great
vision in your discussion

00:42:01.510 --> 00:42:02.470
yesterday.

00:42:02.470 --> 00:42:05.050
I think as a representative of
the manufacturing industry--

00:42:05.050 --> 00:42:09.250
because I look at AB InBev as
one of the largest breweries,

00:42:09.250 --> 00:42:11.710
and you can say it's
representing the manufacturing

00:42:11.710 --> 00:42:14.290
industry, what would
be your takeaway

00:42:14.290 --> 00:42:17.797
be from that perspective
around usage of AI and ML?

00:42:17.797 --> 00:42:18.630
ADAM SPUNBERG: Sure.

00:42:18.630 --> 00:42:21.700
And I do have to
say, you keep saying

00:42:21.700 --> 00:42:23.770
that we did this
great presentation,

00:42:23.770 --> 00:42:25.870
but honestly give
yourself some credit.

00:42:25.870 --> 00:42:28.090
You know, Pluto7 is the
company that really did

00:42:28.090 --> 00:42:29.620
the machine learning and AI.

00:42:29.620 --> 00:42:32.860
We just accepted it and
put it into our system.

00:42:32.860 --> 00:42:34.960
So all credit to you guys.

00:42:34.960 --> 00:42:40.380
But I'd say this, you have to
find use cases that make sense.

00:42:40.380 --> 00:42:43.420
And I think this is
very in line with what

00:42:43.420 --> 00:42:46.780
was being said before,
is that the fact is

00:42:46.780 --> 00:42:49.960
AI is not going to be the right
solution for a lot of things.

00:42:49.960 --> 00:42:52.360
And I think there will
be more and more use

00:42:52.360 --> 00:42:57.100
cases as technology advances
and foundational layers

00:42:57.100 --> 00:42:58.180
of technology.

00:42:58.180 --> 00:43:00.910
So say in our breweries,
the more advanced

00:43:00.910 --> 00:43:05.110
our data collection is, for
example, the more opportunities

00:43:05.110 --> 00:43:08.450
will emerge where we can
do things with that data.

00:43:08.450 --> 00:43:10.000
But it's essential
that you really

00:43:10.000 --> 00:43:11.560
do have the right situations.

00:43:11.560 --> 00:43:15.760
And I think that's where someone
could run after this a little

00:43:15.760 --> 00:43:19.090
irresponsibly and just get so
excited about the AI/machine

00:43:19.090 --> 00:43:22.600
learning idea that, oh,
we can do ML everywhere.

00:43:22.600 --> 00:43:24.820
And that's really not true.

00:43:24.820 --> 00:43:28.180
If you were going to go
swimming in a children's pool,

00:43:28.180 --> 00:43:32.740
you wouldn't say, let's go
buy scuba gear to do that.

00:43:32.740 --> 00:43:35.370
There's certain things-- you
have to find the right setting.

00:43:35.370 --> 00:43:39.400
And I think that's a
combination of, in our case,

00:43:39.400 --> 00:43:42.340
working together with
experts who really

00:43:42.340 --> 00:43:44.050
do know the right situation.

00:43:44.050 --> 00:43:49.030
In the case of Pluto7 with us,
we actually held a hackathon

00:43:49.030 --> 00:43:50.770
where we explored
a number of areas

00:43:50.770 --> 00:43:53.230
where we thought AI
could be valuable.

00:43:53.230 --> 00:43:58.540
And we looked to them to come
up with a model that worked.

00:43:58.540 --> 00:44:01.450
And from those
different options,

00:44:01.450 --> 00:44:04.810
it became apparent that we
had found something here.

00:44:04.810 --> 00:44:09.790
So I think to pursue something
in this space for all of you,

00:44:09.790 --> 00:44:12.970
I would just make sure that
it's a prudent decision.

00:44:12.970 --> 00:44:16.580
Definitely I'm not trying to
stop anybody's excitement.

00:44:16.580 --> 00:44:19.780
Be as passionate as
possible, but save yourself

00:44:19.780 --> 00:44:23.070
the trouble of overdoing it
for the wrong situations.

00:44:23.070 --> 00:44:25.000
SALIL AMONKAR: Thank you Adam.

00:44:25.000 --> 00:44:28.960
And then before we open it
up to the audience, Deepak,

00:44:28.960 --> 00:44:31.360
final thoughts from
you as a representative

00:44:31.360 --> 00:44:34.655
of the retail industry and
a high growth one at that.

00:44:34.655 --> 00:44:39.030
DEEPAK MEHROTRA: I wouldn't go
as far as representing retail,

00:44:39.030 --> 00:44:41.650
that Mr. Bezos
would be better off.

00:44:41.650 --> 00:44:47.950
But I'm thinking like ML/AI
is like a little baby.

00:44:47.950 --> 00:44:51.160
So you start putting
in data, you still

00:44:51.160 --> 00:44:53.740
have to feed and
change the diapers.

00:44:53.740 --> 00:44:56.260
And that's where
we are right now.

00:44:56.260 --> 00:44:58.390
We are hoping that
in a few years,

00:44:58.390 --> 00:44:59.680
this baby is going to grow up.

00:44:59.680 --> 00:45:01.270
And she's going to run.

00:45:01.270 --> 00:45:04.210
And she's going to do pole
vault, which I never did.

00:45:04.210 --> 00:45:07.750
And she's going to win Olympic
medals, which I couldn't even

00:45:07.750 --> 00:45:08.650
dream of.

00:45:08.650 --> 00:45:13.660
So this is pretty much how
we are looking at ML and AI.

00:45:13.660 --> 00:45:17.560
So it's with us,
we have to pick--

00:45:17.560 --> 00:45:22.930
with this, we are driven to
ensure that we think future,

00:45:22.930 --> 00:45:29.620
we set up systems, data
flow, rethink processes.

00:45:29.620 --> 00:45:35.746
And then, the planning in a
box that we did with Pluto7, we

00:45:35.746 --> 00:45:37.870
were able to see some good
results as far as demand

00:45:37.870 --> 00:45:41.320
forecast and supply chain
optimizing is concerned.

00:45:41.320 --> 00:45:45.040
So we press a button,
we see what's going on,

00:45:45.040 --> 00:45:47.760
we quickly change what's
in the supply chain.

00:45:47.760 --> 00:45:49.880
I mean, it's still basic.

00:45:49.880 --> 00:45:51.670
There is a speed now.

00:45:51.670 --> 00:45:56.500
So 500, 1,000, 2,000 SKUs
that we have to manage.

00:45:56.500 --> 00:46:00.740
We are able to do it
quick and really fast.

00:46:00.740 --> 00:46:03.910
Now, when will this baby grow
up and just take all the charge?

00:46:03.910 --> 00:46:05.130
Well, we'll see when.

00:46:05.130 --> 00:46:06.762
SALIL AMONKAR:
Thank you, Deepak.

00:46:06.762 --> 00:46:11.270
I think with that, I'd like to
open this up to the audience.

00:46:11.270 --> 00:46:12.930
We have about five minutes left.

00:46:19.720 --> 00:46:22.630
AUDIENCE: Is there
any packages that--

00:46:22.630 --> 00:46:25.540
package applications
out there that can help

00:46:25.540 --> 00:46:28.450
jumpstart your application?

00:46:28.450 --> 00:46:30.679
SUDHAKAR PARAKALA: It's
more for you, I guess.

00:46:30.679 --> 00:46:31.845
We didn't use any packaged--

00:46:31.845 --> 00:46:35.740
SALIL AMONKAR: OK, I think
I'll leave it to Deepak.

00:46:35.740 --> 00:46:37.920
Yes, Deepak, the
question is, is there

00:46:37.920 --> 00:46:39.660
any packaged
application out there

00:46:39.660 --> 00:46:42.545
that can help jumpstart
their business?

00:46:42.545 --> 00:46:45.600
DEEPAK MEHROTRA: Well, it'll
sound like unsponsored ad,

00:46:45.600 --> 00:46:51.990
but we used planning in a box
that Pluto7 has, which uses,

00:46:51.990 --> 00:46:54.290
of course, Google
Cloud and ML and AI,

00:46:54.290 --> 00:46:57.450
things within the
Google ecosystem.

00:46:57.450 --> 00:47:01.440
That did help us a lot
as far as our inventory

00:47:01.440 --> 00:47:02.730
controls are concerned.

00:47:02.730 --> 00:47:10.410
So with much very fast demand,
very decent demand forecasting,

00:47:10.410 --> 00:47:12.650
and a quick connection
to supply chain,

00:47:12.650 --> 00:47:19.010
we were able to see very
visible inventory reductions.

00:47:19.010 --> 00:47:22.688
AUDIENCE: And then help move
fast movers to slow movers?

00:47:22.688 --> 00:47:24.938
DEEPAK MEHROTRA: Can you
repeat your question, please?

00:47:24.938 --> 00:47:29.384
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] fast
movers and slow movers.

00:47:32.167 --> 00:47:33.125
DEEPAK MEHROTRA: Right.

00:47:35.650 --> 00:47:38.290
I think more with
the fast movers.

00:47:38.290 --> 00:47:41.710
Because again, we as a
company, our slow movers

00:47:41.710 --> 00:47:43.100
are really slow.

00:47:43.100 --> 00:47:45.710
So one of the disadvantages,
as we were talking earlier,

00:47:45.710 --> 00:47:48.700
you need to have data to
run some of these models

00:47:48.700 --> 00:47:50.450
and see real results.

00:47:50.450 --> 00:47:54.640
So our slow movers are
really slow movers.

00:47:54.640 --> 00:47:58.180
We didn't expect actually
some of the results

00:47:58.180 --> 00:48:02.360
to be benefiting the slow-moving
SKUs and what have you.

00:48:02.360 --> 00:48:05.260
So we kept our safety
levels in that case

00:48:05.260 --> 00:48:08.920
and worried more about
what was really selling

00:48:08.920 --> 00:48:12.912
and where we had growth.

00:48:12.912 --> 00:48:14.331
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:48:14.331 --> 00:48:16.064
DEEPAK MEHROTRA: Sure.

00:48:16.064 --> 00:48:17.480
SALIL AMONKAR: Any
other question?

00:48:23.260 --> 00:48:23.760
Great.

00:48:23.760 --> 00:48:27.500
If not, I think
that's pretty much it.

00:48:27.500 --> 00:48:30.280
Feel free to drop by
our booth at Pluto7.

00:48:30.280 --> 00:48:32.840
I have put it up there.

00:48:32.840 --> 00:48:35.917
We can show you more demos on
what's possible with AI and ML.

00:48:35.917 --> 00:48:36.750
Thank you very much.

00:48:36.750 --> 00:48:39.800
[THEME MUSIC PLAYING]

